---
import { PLATFORM } from "../lib/brand";

/**
 * THE AIRTABLE MARK, IN TWO PLACES, SO THE CLIENT CAN PICK ONE.
 *
 * Dan, on the splash review call: *"we could either leave it here, but it would be good to put the
 * Airtable logo on there just for visibility, reference, credibility, to put that there. And I was
 * wondering if maybe it should go in the upper right, like 'Airtable edition' or something over
 * here. So I don't know which one looks better."* He named two placements and could not choose, so
 * both were built and shown behind `?logo=`. Oleh chose this one on 2026-08-28 and the other came
 * out: the full Airtable logo, icon AND wordmark, standing in the right column, level with the top
 * of the headline's first line and flush to the column's right edge, with "edition" set under it
 * in our own face. The one that lost put a bare icon after an "Airtable edition" line inside the
 * headline; with this lockup present that line was the page saying the same two words twice.
 *                             column of the split, its top edge level with the top of the
 *                             headline's first line, with "edition" set under it in Urbanist.
 *
 * ── WHAT CHANGED IN B, AND WHY (2026-08-28) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * B used to be a 1.125rem square glyph plus an 11px "AIRTABLE EDITION" micro-label, pinned to the
 * top right of the page column under the review chip strip. Dan's verdict: *"I don't like the logo
 * in the corner, it's too small and too much in the corner. I'd put it level with where the main
 * headline starts. And I'd like to try taking the full logo with the word 'Airtable' in it, and
 * write 'Edition' in our own font underneath. And make it a bit bigger. Maybe later we fade it so
 * it doesn't contrast so much, but not now — for now we just align it."*
 *
 * So: the real horizontal logo, in the right column beside the headline rather than in the page
 * corner, bigger, and the word "edition" set in OUR face under it. NOT faded — he said not yet, and
 * nothing here reduces its contrast.
 *
 * ── WHERE THE WORDMARK CAME FROM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Airtable's brand guidelines (https://www.airtable.com/company/trademark-guidelines) say there are
 * "two approved lockups of our icon and wordmark, which you may download here", and "here" is a
 * shared Airtable base view — a JS app whose rows only arrive over an authenticated XHR this
 * sandbox will not make. So the asset was taken from the other place Airtable publishes it: the
 * lockup in the header of www.airtable.com itself, which is that same artwork, live.
 *
 * NOTHING IS REDRAWN. The wordmark path is Airtable's own vector, lifted verbatim out of their
 * nav's `<svg width="94" height="20">`. The icon is the four-colour mark this repo already carried.
 * The SPACING between the two is not invented either: Airtable's own header lockup was measured in
 * a live browser, ink box to ink box, and those ratios are what the file uses —
 *
 *     icon ink height   : wordmark ink height   =  27.859 : 19.258  = 1.4466
 *     gap between them  : wordmark ink height   =   6.875 : 19.258  = 0.3570
 *     both centred on one horizontal axis (their ink centres agree to 0.13px)
 *
 * The result is `/logos/airtable-lockup-{light,dark}.svg`, `viewBox="0 0 133.19 27.859"`, whose ink
 * bounding box fills the viewBox exactly — no padding to reason about, so a CSS height is the ink
 * height. The full arithmetic is in the comment at the head of each file.
 *
 * TWO FILES, ONE PER THEME, WHICH THE PLAIN GLYPH DID NOT NEED. Every path in `airtable.svg` is
 * saturated brand colour, so one file held on both floors. A wordmark is INK: Airtable set theirs in
 * `#181D26`, which disappears on this app's `#0a0a0a`. So this follows `Credibility.astro` exactly —
 * a `-light`/`-dark` pair, one of them always `display: none`, the swap written in both directions
 * because the stored theme stamps `data-theme` and the default setting stamps nothing. The icon is
 * identical in both files; only the wordmark's fill changes, `#181D26` ↔ `#FFFFFF`, which is the
 * reversed treatment Airtable's own guidelines list ("color, white, dark").
 *
 * ── SIZE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `Credibility.astro` sizes each partner logo so its WORDMARK ink lands at one shared height,
 * because that is the thing the eye compares when it decides two logos are "the same size". Same
 * method here, and the same arithmetic — target height divided by the wordmark's share of the file:
 *
 *     wordmark share of this lockup      19.258 / 27.859      = 0.6913
 *     target wordmark ink height         var(--bo-t-24)       = 1.5rem
 *     ------------------------------------------------------------------
 *     logo height                        1.5 / 0.6913         = 2.17rem   (→ 10.375rem wide)
 *
 * WHY 1.5rem FOR THE WORDMARK. It is `--bo-t-24`, the top of this page's non-display ladder and the
 * rung both `.splash-rail-h` ("What you get") and `/thanks` give a SECTION HEADING. That is exactly
 * this thing's rank: it is the second-loudest statement in the split, under an h1 that is 5rem and
 * over a ladder caption that is 14px. It is also 1.58x the 0.95rem the three partner logos get at
 * the foot of this page, which is the difference between the platform this edition is about and the
 * three names that run it — a difference the page should be able to state without a caption.
 *
 * The old stamp was an 18px SQUARE. This is a 35px-tall, 166px-wide lockup: nearly twice the height
 * and nine times the area, which is "a bit bigger" honestly read. It is also comfortably over the
 * 120px minimum width Airtable's guidance gives for the full lockup, at every width this page has —
 * the logo does not shrink below 62rem, because the column it sits in stops being a column there.
 *
 * ── "edition", IN OUR OWN FACE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `--bo-t-11` / 700 / `0.12em` / uppercase / `--bo-muted`. That is not a new treatment: it is this
 * page's ONE micro-label voice, the `.bo-eyebrow` recipe, and it is already doing this exact job in
 * `Credibility.astro` — `.cred__role` sets "RUNS THE SURVEY" under each partner's logo in it. The
 * word under a logo, in muted spaced caps, is a thing this page already knows how to say.
 *
 * It has to be unmistakably ours rather than part of their mark, and every axis of it differs from
 * the wordmark directly above: Urbanist against Airtable's own face, 11px against a 24px wordmark,
 * spaced caps against tight sentence case, `--bo-muted` against full-contrast ink. Setting it any
 * larger starts to read as the second word of a two-word logotype, which is the one thing it must
 * not do. It sits `--bo-space-1` under the lockup, close enough to be read as its annotation.
 *
 * ── FLUSH RIGHT, BOTH OF THEM (2026-08-28, second pass) ───────────────────────────────────────
 * Dan, on seeing it in place: *"I'd put the logo on the right — right-align it, and 'edition'
 * right-aligned too. Basically drag it as far right as it goes, that'll look better."*
 *
 * So the column's flex alignment flips end-wards and the whole lockup goes to the right edge of
 * the page's content column — the same edge the ladder and its caption below already end on, so
 * the three now share one margin instead of two. "edition" goes with it, and because the wordmark
 * is the rightmost ink in the file (the lockup's ink box fills its viewBox exactly, with no
 * trailing padding), flush-right puts the word under the last letter of "Airtable" rather than
 * under the icon. That is the alignment he asked for and it is also the better one: the word now
 * hangs off the type it annotates instead of off a picture two inches to its left.
 *
 * ── NO SCRIPT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every element here is server-rendered, so the scoped styles below actually reach it. A mark built
 * with `createElement` or `innerHTML` would never carry this component's `data-astro-cid-*` and
 * every rule in this block would silently miss — this repo has paid for that lesson three times.
 */

/**
 * The horizontal lockup, built from Airtable's own icon and wordmark at their own measured
 * geometry. Source and arithmetic are in the docblock above and in each file's head comment.
 */
const LOCKUP_LIGHT = "/logos/airtable-lockup-light.svg";
const LOCKUP_DARK = "/logos/airtable-lockup-dark.svg";

/** The file is 133.19 x 27.859. Scaled to integers at the same ratio so the box is reserved before
    the SVG loads; `width: auto` in CSS then takes the real ratio from the file itself. */
const LOCKUP_W = 478;
const LOCKUP_H = 100;
---

<p class="edm-standing">
      {/* Two files, one always hidden, exactly as `Credibility.astro` does it. The alternative is
          one file and a CSS filter, which would repaint the icon's yellow, blue and red along with
          the wordmark's ink. The visible one carries the platform name as its `alt` and the span
          below adds "edition", so the pair is announced once, as "Airtable edition". */}
      <img
        class="edm-standing__logo edm-standing__logo--light"
        src={LOCKUP_LIGHT}
        alt={PLATFORM}
        width={LOCKUP_W}
        height={LOCKUP_H}
      />
      <img
        class="edm-standing__logo edm-standing__logo--dark"
        src={LOCKUP_DARK}
        alt=""
        aria-hidden="true"
        width={LOCKUP_W}
        height={LOCKUP_H}
      />
      <span class="edm-standing__word">edition</span>
    </p>

<style>
  /* PLACEMENT B. This is now a block INSIDE the split's right column — `HomeOpening.astro` renders
     it above the score ladder — rather than a flex child of `main` asking for the page's right
     edge. That is the whole point of the rework: it is not in the corner any more, it is level with
     the headline, and the alignment is a consequence of where it sits in the grid rather than
     something an offset has to keep true. `HomeOpening.astro` carries the row geometry and the one
     measured offset; everything in here is the lockup itself.

     `margin: 0` because the column, not this element, owns the space around it. */
  .edm-standing {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* FLUSH RIGHT. This element is stretched to the full width of the split's right column by the
       flex column it sits in, so `flex-end` lands the lockup on that column's right edge, which is
       the page's content edge and the edge the ladder and its caption below already end on. It
       carries "edition" with it: the span is shrink-to-fit, so the same alignment puts the word
       under the wordmark's last letter rather than under the icon. `text-align` would not do this
       — the span's box is only as wide as the word. */
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: var(--bo-space-1);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── STACKED, IT CENTRES ON ITS OWN RULE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Oleh, 2026-08-31: *"let's put Airtable Edition on that line, centred in the middle."*

     Flush right is correct inside the split, where this shares an edge with the ladder and the
     caption below it and that shared edge IS the composition. Stacked there is no right column to
     belong to: the rule above runs the full width, and a mark hugging one end of a full-width rule
     reads as left over rather than as placed.

     `align-items`, not `text-align`, for the same reason the flush-right rule gives — this is a
     flex column and "edition" is a shrink-to-fit span, so this is what moves the image and the word
     together and keeps the word centred under the WORDMARK rather than under the icon.

     THE RULE LIVES HERE AND NOT IN `HomeOpening`, and that is not a preference. It was written
     there first and did nothing: a parent's scoped `<style>` cannot reach inside a child component,
     because the parent's rules carry the parent's `data-astro-cid-*` and these elements carry this
     file's. Fifth time this app has hit that trap. Anything that styles `.edm-standing` belongs in
     this file. */
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .edm-standing {
      align-items: center;
    }
  }

  /* THE ONE NUMBER, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS DERIVED FROM IT. `--edm-ink` is the height the WORDMARK's
     ink should come out at; the file's height is that divided by the wordmark's 0.6913 share of it.
     1.5rem is `--bo-t-24`, the top of this page's non-default type ladder — see the docblock.

     It is a custom property so a caller can resize the whole lockup by setting one value instead of
     recomputing the divide. `HomeOpening` does that on a phone, where this is a masthead mark
     beside an eyebrow rather than a standing object beside a headline.

     `rem`, not `em`: this is a standing object in a column of its own, not a mark riding a line of
     fluid display type, and it must not resize with a headline it is merely level with. */
  /* A FALLBACK, NOT A DECLARATION. Setting `--edm-ink` on `.edm-standing` itself would BEAT the
     value a caller inherits down to it — a property set on the element always wins over one coming
     from an ancestor — so the override would silently do nothing. That is exactly what happened on
     the first attempt: `HomeOpening` asked for a smaller mark on a phone and got the full-size one.
     The default belongs in the `var()` fallback, where an inherited value can reach it. */
  .edm-standing__logo {
    height: calc(var(--edm-ink, var(--bo-t-24)) / 0.6913);
    width: auto;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  /* Our annotation of their mark, in our face. `.bo-eyebrow`'s recipe, muted — the same thing
     `.cred__role` does under the three partner logos at the foot of this page. */
  .edm-standing__word {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* THE THEME SWAP IS EXPLICIT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, for the reason `Credibility.astro` spells out:
     the stored choice stamps `data-theme` on the root and the default "system" setting stamps
     nothing, so the media query has to answer for system and the attribute has to be able to
     overrule it. Defining either alone leaves one of the four combinations showing the wrong file.

     `display: block`, not `flex`: these are the flex ITEMS, and the column above lays them out. */
  .edm-standing__logo--dark {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) .edm-standing__logo--light {
      display: none;
    }
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) .edm-standing__logo--dark {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  :root[data-theme="dark"] .edm-standing__logo--light {
    display: none;
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .edm-standing__logo--dark {
    display: block;
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .edm-standing__logo--light {
    display: block;
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] .edm-standing__logo--dark {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
